## 9. Method B — Multiple barcodes from one photograph

I also want to upload or take one photograph containing multiple book barcodes.

The system should attempt to identify all readable barcodes in that photograph.

Example:
- Place five books on a table.
- Position their back covers so their barcodes are visible.
- Take one photograph.
- Detect all readable barcode identifiers.
- Add all unique valid detections to the batch queue.

### Implementation requirements

- Use a multi-symbol decoding path.
- Retrieve all decoded symbols rather than only the first.
- Increase the symbol limit from the existing `maxNumberOfSymbols: 6` where technically appropriate.
- Evaluate each decoded barcode independently.
- Preserve all successful detections even when another barcode in the same photograph is invalid.
- Avoid adding the same decoded number twice from one image.
- Clearly report how many barcodes were found and how many were valid.

Use the existing image preprocessing and barcode decoding pipeline where possible.

If a photograph is too blurry, explain that and allow another photograph or manual entry.

Do not guarantee that every barcode in a photograph can be decoded. Image quality, barcode size, orientation, glare, and overlap can affect detection.

The most important requirement is to preserve every successful detection rather than stopping after the first.

